      "text": "The now classic book, Fried Green Tomatoes at the Whistle Stop Café (Flagg, 1988), and the 1991 movie version, Fried Green Tomatoes, reveal the value and meaning of friendship. Intertwined stories follow Evelyn and Ninny, middle-aged and older women in the 1980s developing a close relationship after meeting by chance, with flashbacks to the story of Idgie and Ruth, two young adult women in the 1920s looking after and caring about one another. Themes of affection, companionship, emotional support, solace, practical assistance, loneliness, and health concerns run through the accounts of both pairs of friends and the larger social networks in which they are embedded. These are the same themes researchers have studied while documenting the roles of friends in contemporary adult life.\n\nWhat Is Friendship?",
