      "href": "readings/park-mcdonough-2013/full.html",
      "text": "As pointed out in the most recent Handbook of the Psychology of Aging, many theories of cognitive aging, even local theories of memory, attention, executive function, emotion regulation, well-being, personality, are incorporating multiple aspects of the neurological substrate in their research programs (Dixon, 2011, pp. 16). Recent cognitive theories of aging have neural compensation as a basic component, invoking mechanisms such as cognitive reserve (Stern, 2009), functional plasticity (Greenwood, 2007), and neural scaffolding (Park & Reuter-Lorenz, 2009). Such strong views of neural plasticity have resulted in a resurgence of interest in cognitive training research designed to improve cognition through enhancement of neural structures or reorganization of functional circuitry (Lustig, Shah, Seidler, & Reuter-Lorenz, 2009). Functional imaging of the aging mind has provided a fundamental",
